WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.210
Our head teacher wants an agent

2
00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:03.110
for the school office.

3
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.770
It should answer parents, check

4
00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:07.750
attendance and send the weekly news.

5
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.900
Someone said: give it every

6
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.100
tool, so it never gets stuck.

7
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:14.500
Good design?

8
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.220
Today: designing an agent for a school

9
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.980
job, with the fewest tools and clear

10
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:21.340
roles.

11
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.620
Good design starts from the

12
00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:26.120
job, not the tools.

13
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.960
Who will use it, and what should it do?

14
00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:33.530
And what would a mistake cost: a wrong

15
00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.850
reminder, or a wrong message to a parent?

16
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.430
Then give it only the

17
00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.650
tools that job needs.

18
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.700
An agent acts only through its

19
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.820
tools, so every extra tool is

20
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.080
another way to go wrong.

21
00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:50.890
A homework helper needs the timetable

22
00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:53.030
and the library, nothing more.

23
00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:57.370
This is called least privilege: the fewest

24
00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.650
tools and the least data the job needs.

25
00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.060
If the agent misreads a

26
00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.060
request, it can’t do much harm.

27
00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.170
It’s the rule we already use

28
00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:07.070
for staff accounts.

29
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:10.800
Next, split the work into roles.

30
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.160
The planner, the model,

31
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.280
proposes each tool call.

32
00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.270
A checker, a few strict rules in

33
00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.450
code, reviews it before it runs.

34
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.460
The app runs it, and a person

35
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:24.940
approves any action.

36
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.680
The team lab’s checker has five rules.

37
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:30.700
The tool is on.

38
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.300
Each detail has the right shape.

39
00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.390
Every name and number came from

40
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:38.350
your words or a result.

41
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.640
No earlier step failed.

42
00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.970
And a person said yes to any action.

43
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:45.830
Quick check!

44
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.520
An office agent only answers

45
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.460
parents’ questions about homework.

46
00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.460
Which tools should it get?

47
00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.510
The timetable and the library.

48
00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.050
Nothing in that job sends

49
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:01.970
messages or reads attendance.

50
00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.800
If parents later want replies by

51
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.520
message, add that tool then, with a

52
00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.020
person approving each one.

53
00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:11.890
Write the permissions down as a

54
00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:13.890
table, one line per tool.

55
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.300
Here, the weather and the

56
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.680
timetable run freely, while

57
00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.360
reminders and messages wait for a yes.

58
00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.960
Any tool that isn’t in the table is off.

59
00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:27.920
In the app, send the weather

60
00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.980
request: the checker passes it.

61
00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.810
Then ask what nine Z has

62
00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.570
tomorrow: Darsi Tiny writes nine

63
00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:37.430
C, and the checker stops it.

64
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.200
Turn the checker off and try again.

65
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:41.920
Your turn.

66
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.310
Who should approve the weekly news

67
00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:47.830
before the agent sends it to parents?

68
00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.510
A named person at school, every time.

69
00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.130
Remember: start from the job

70
00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:55.690
and what a mistake costs.

71
00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:58.630
Give the fewest tools, in a

72
00:02:58.630 --> 00:02:59.570
permissions table.

73
00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.730
Split the work into roles, and a

74
00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.070
person approves actions.

75
00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:06.710
Try it in class tomorrow:

76
00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:08.510
run a design sprint.

77
00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.440
Groups pick a school job and write a

78
00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.280
one-page design: the job, the tools, who

79
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.620
approves, and how we’ll know it works.

80
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.120
Then they swap and hunt for an extra tool.
